WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.039
This lesson sits between Newton's second and third laws, so it is easy for learners to blend them accidentally.

2
00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:12.078
For one object, the response depends on the net force and the mass.

3
00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:18.117
That is Newton's second-law structure.

4
00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:24.156
For two interacting objects, the pair forces are equal and opposite on different objects.

5
00:00:24.156 --> 00:00:30.195
That is Newton's third-law structure.

6
00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:36.235
The biggest win of the lesson is knowing which story you are in before you start the reasoning.

7
00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:42.274
Keep this takeaway in view: First decide whether the question is about response of one object or interaction between two objects.

8
00:00:42.274 --> 00:00:48.313
One-object question means F = ma.

9
00:00:48.313 --> 00:00:54.352
Use net force and mass to predict the object's acceleration.

10
00:00:54.352 --> 00:01:00.391
Two-object interaction means equal and opposite pair.

11
00:01:00.391 --> 00:01:06.430
The force pair matches in size and opposes in direction on different objects.

12
00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:11.761
Here the same Master Arrow acts on two different crafts.

13
00:01:11.761 --> 00:01:17.092
The lighter craft shows a larger Motion Shift.

14
00:01:17.092 --> 00:01:22.423
The heavier craft shows a smaller Motion Shift.

15
00:01:22.423 --> 00:01:27.754
Nothing mysterious has happened to the force.

16
00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:33.086
The difference comes from the mass.

17
00:01:33.086 --> 00:01:38.417
This picture is powerful because it lets learners see that equal force does not force equal acceleration when the resisting mass is different.

18
00:01:38.417 --> 00:01:43.748
Keep this takeaway in view: The response size depends on both the net force and the mass, not on force alone.

19
00:01:43.748 --> 00:01:49.079
Same force: Both crafts experience the same Master Arrow.

20
00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:54.410
Lighter craft: Less mass means the same net force produces a bigger Motion Shift.

21
00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:59.797
Imagine the same tow cable pulling two different sleds.

22
00:01:59.797 --> 00:02:05.183
The cable tension can match in both stories, but the lighter sled responds more because less mass resists the motion change.

23
00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:10.570
This analogy is useful because it preserves the force equality while allowing the acceleration outcomes to differ.

24
00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:15.957
That is exactly the distinction learners need before the worked example begins.

25
00:02:15.957 --> 00:02:21.343
Keep this takeaway in view: Equal force and equal acceleration are not the same statement.

26
00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:26.730
Tow cable means interaction force.

27
00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:32.117
The pull itself can be equal in different cases.

28
00:02:32.117 --> 00:02:37.503
Light sled means larger acceleration.

29
00:02:37.503 --> 00:02:42.890
Less mass means the same pull causes more motion change.

30
00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:48.156
The second-law relation is acceleration equals net force divided by mass.

31
00:02:48.156 --> 00:02:53.423
In words, the Motion Shift comes from the Master Arrow shared across the object's mass.

32
00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:58.689
A larger Master Arrow gives a larger acceleration if the mass stays fixed.

33
00:02:58.689 --> 00:03:03.955
A larger mass gives a smaller acceleration if the net force stays fixed.

34
00:03:03.955 --> 00:03:09.222
This is the clean response model for one-object questions.

35
00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:14.488
Keep this takeaway in view: For one object, acceleration belongs to the net force and the mass together.

36
00:03:14.488 --> 00:03:19.755
Bigger net force: If mass stays the same, acceleration becomes larger.

37
00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:25.021
Bigger mass: If net force stays the same, acceleration becomes smaller.

38
00:03:25.021 --> 00:03:30.287
a = F_net / m.

39
00:03:30.287 --> 00:03:35.554
F_net = ma.

40
00:03:35.554 --> 00:03:40.820
Use the Master Arrow, or resultant force, not just one listed force from the scene.

41
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:46.375
Newton's third law says interacting objects exert equal and opposite forces on each other.

42
00:03:46.375 --> 00:03:51.930
Those two forces belong to different objects.

43
00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:57.485
So they are not the pair you cancel when finding one object's net force.

44
00:03:57.485 --> 00:04:03.040
To find one object's acceleration, use only the forces acting on that one object and combine them into its own Master Arrow.

45
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.595
This distinction protects the learner from saying the heavier object pushes back harder simply because it accelerates less.

46
00:04:08.595 --> 00:04:14.150
Keep this takeaway in view: Third-law equality and second-law response live in different reasoning boxes.

47
00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:19.705
Third-law pair means different objects.

48
00:04:19.705 --> 00:04:25.260
Equal and opposite interaction forces act on two separate bodies.

49
00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.815
Net force question means one object only.

50
00:04:30.815 --> 00:04:36.370
Use only the forces acting on the chosen object.

51
00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:42.792
A common trap is to look at the smaller acceleration of the heavier craft and decide that the interaction forces must be different.

52
00:04:42.792 --> 00:04:49.214
That is wrong.

53
00:04:49.214 --> 00:04:55.637
The interaction forces can still be equal and opposite.

54
00:04:55.637 --> 00:05:02.059
The difference in acceleration comes from the difference in mass.

55
00:05:02.059 --> 00:05:08.481
Another trap is to place a third-law pair into one object's net-force balance and cancel it there.

56
00:05:08.481 --> 00:05:14.903
The force pair acts on different objects, so that cancellation is not allowed in a one-object response question.

57
00:05:14.903 --> 00:05:21.326
Keep this takeaway in view: Mass explains the response difference; it does not break force-pair equality.

58
00:05:21.326 --> 00:05:27.748
Equal interaction forces can still produce different accelerations.

59
00:05:27.748 --> 00:05:34.170
Different accelerations come from different masses under the same net force.

60
00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:38.179
Check yourself.

61
00:05:38.179 --> 00:05:42.188
For a one-object response question, which relation do you use?

62
00:05:42.188 --> 00:05:46.198
A equals F net over m.

63
00:05:46.198 --> 00:05:50.207
If two objects interact, what does Newton's third law tell you?

64
00:05:50.207 --> 00:05:54.216
The forces are equal and opposite on different objects.

65
00:05:54.216 --> 00:05:58.225
If one object accelerates less under the same net force, what changed?

66
00:05:58.225 --> 00:06:02.235
Its mass is larger.

67
00:06:02.235 --> 00:06:06.244
Those three checks are the spine of the lesson.

68
00:06:06.244 --> 00:06:10.253
Keep this takeaway in view: Response and interaction must be sorted before the arithmetic begins.

69
00:06:10.253 --> 00:06:14.262
Response model means a = F_net / m.

70
00:06:14.262 --> 00:06:18.272
Use this for one-object acceleration questions.

71
00:06:18.272 --> 00:06:22.281
Interaction model means equal and opposite pair.

72
00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:26.290
Use this for force-pair reasoning between objects.

73
00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:32.513
You are ready for the worked example now.

74
00:06:32.513 --> 00:06:38.735
The same eighteen newton Master Arrow acts east on a three kilogram scout and a six kilogram freighter.

75
00:06:38.735 --> 00:06:44.958
Use a equals F net over m for each craft separately.

76
00:06:44.958 --> 00:06:51.180
For the scout, eighteen divided by three gives six metres per second squared east.

77
00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:57.403
For the freighter, eighteen divided by six gives three metres per second squared east.

78
00:06:57.403 --> 00:07:03.625
The scout accelerates more because the same Master Arrow is shared across less mass.

79
00:07:03.625 --> 00:07:09.848
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: same net force, different mass, different acceleration.

80
00:07:09.848 --> 00:07:16.070
Step 1: Recognize this as a one-object response question for each craft.

81
00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:22.292
Step 2: Use a = F_net / m separately for each mass.

82
00:07:22.292 --> 00:07:28.515
scout: a = 18 / 3 = 6 m/s^2 east.

83
00:07:28.515 --> 00:07:34.738
freighter: a = 18 / 6 = 3 m/s^2 east.

84
00:07:34.738 --> 00:07:40.960
The same Master Arrow produces different Motion Shifts because the masses differ.
